"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][18]={"university_physics_en:M18-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M18-L1 – Experiment Planning, Laboratory Rules and Measurement Strategy<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Bachelor: Physics Laboratory Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M16: uncertainty and data analysis;\nM17: model comparison. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate a testable measurement\nquestion, document a risk decision and design a measurement plan with\ncontrolled quantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>A laboratory experiment starts before any instrument is switched on:\nWhich quantity is to be estimated or which physical relation is to be\ntested, and under what assumptions? A testable hypothesis specifies an\nobservable relation, such as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>∝<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2\\propto l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an ideal pendulum at small amplitude. Define the independent\nvariable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nobservable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand potential confounders including amplitude, temperature, air motion\nand timer resolution. The plan distinguishes calibrations, controls and\nthe actual measurement series. Predict orders of magnitude in advance;\nthis helps catch unit errors and inappropriate measurement ranges. All\nnumerical data in this module are original teaching examples, not\nlaboratory observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>Local laboratory rules, risk assessments and supervisor instructions\ntake precedence over general course material. Electrical, optical,\nmagnetic, hot, pressurised and radiation-related equipment must only be\nused after approval and with the required controls. Record the hazard,\nprotective measure, stop condition and responsible person before\nstarting. A nominally low-energy measurement is not automatically safe\nwhen the device configuration is unknown. Stop when abnormal noise,\nodour, overheating or missing guards are observed. This lesson teaches\nplanning; it is not a site-specific operating procedure.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msqrt><mfrac><mi>l<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>g<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>l<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≪<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi\\sqrt{\\frac{l}{g}},\\qquad T^2=\\frac{4\\pi^2}{g}l\\quad(\\theta\\ll1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Example: A pendulum with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l=0.80\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a small-angle period near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.79<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.79\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInstead of timing one swing, one may, under safe and approved\nconditions, time ten cycles and divide by ten. With unchanged absolute\nstart/stop error this reduces the relative timing uncertainty. Record\nthe number of cycles and amplitude range, however: more cycles do not\nautomatically correct systematic deviations from the model.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_087_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Flowchart: hypothesis, safety, calibration, measurement and analysis\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Flowchart: hypothesis, safety,\ncalibration, measurement and analysis<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Five stages in an approved measurement workflow<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name an independent, dependent and confounding variable in a\npendulum experiment.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Estimate the small-angle period for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l=0.80\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give four elements of a documented risk decision.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nperiod\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand for example swing amplitude.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.80<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9.81<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.79<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi\\sqrt{0.80/9.81}\\,\\mathrm s\\approx1.79\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Hazard, mitigation, stop criterion and responsible person.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>Before analysis, record instrument identity, calibration date where\navailable, conditions, raw-data format and the intended uncertainty\ntreatment. A defensible plan includes repeats, at least one control\npoint and a predeclared rule for excluding observations. Finally ask\nwhether the experiment could actually falsify its hypothesis: if every\nconceivable outcome can be redescribed afterwards as agreement, the\nresearch question needs sharpening.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>A credible measurement starts with a hypothesis, safety, calibration\nand a traceable data plan.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Mechanics Experiments: Moment of\nInertia and Oscillation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://gpr.physik.hu-berlin.de/Praktikumsordnung/Praktikumsordnung.html\">Humboldt-Universität zu Berlin – Praktikumsordnung<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 2 sec. 1–2, lines 13–20; table of contents § 5; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L1 – Experiment Planning, Laboratory Rules and Measurement Strategy · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://praktikum.physik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de/versuchsbeschreibungen-physik/\">Ruhr-Universität Bochum – Versuchsbeschreibungen Physik<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · A5 lines 40–61; D3 lines 212–230; C14/C15 lines 455–499; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L1 – Experiment Planning, Laboratory Rules and Measurement Strategy · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M18-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M18-L2 – Mechanics Experiments: Moment of Inertia and Oscillation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Bachelor: Physics Laboratory Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M3: rotation; M9: analytical\nmechanics; M16: uncertainty. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine a moment of inertia\nfrom a calibrated oscillation model and test the model assumptions\nagainst data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>Rotational dynamics relates applied torque to the body’s moment of\ninertia\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is not an intrinsic material constant: mass distribution and the\nrotation axis matter. A uniform thin disk about its symmetry axis has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=MR^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne practical determination uses torsional oscillations. For small angle\nand an approximately linear restoring torque\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=-\\kappa\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe motion is harmonic. Analysis must separate the inertia of the\napparatus from that of the specimen.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>The equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\ddot\\varphi+\\kappa\\varphi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nimplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>κ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi\\sqrt{I_{\\mathrm{tot}}/\\kappa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the period of the empty holder with inertia\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith an added specimen, subtraction of squared periods yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\mathrm{sample}}=\\kappa(T^2-T_0^2)/(4\\pi^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided the torsional stiffness and bearing state are unchanged. The\ndifference removes constant holder inertia. Damping, large amplitudes,\nstiffness drift or an off-axis mounting limit this simplified equation.\nCalibrate the stiffness independently or against a known reference.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>κ<\/mi><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>M<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\mathrm{sample}}=\\frac{\\kappa}{4\\pi^2}(T^2-T_0^2),\\qquad I_{\\mathrm{disk}}=\\tfrac12MR^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>A synthetic reference disk has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=0.20\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.15\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ideal disk formula predicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.00225<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.00225\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.025<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=0.025\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m/rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe holder-free period is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.88<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.88\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReal rigs almost always contribute holder inertia, so that number must\nnot be compared directly with the observed period of the full assembly.\nFor known reference inertias\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\mathrm{ref}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2=(4\\pi^2/\\kappa)(I_{\\mathrm{ref}}+I_0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPlotting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\mathrm{ref}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>κ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=4\\pi^2/\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand intercept\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=aI_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ntherefore the holder inertia is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_0=b/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_298_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Linear relationship between period squared and moment of inertia with holder contribution\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Linear relationship between period\nsquared and moment of inertia with holder contribution<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Period squared versus known inertia<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=0.20\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.15\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do we subtract\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_0^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which geometric uncertainty enters the disk formula with a factor of\ntwo?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0.15<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.00225<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.5\\cdot0.20\\cdot0.15^2=0.00225\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis proportional to the total inertia whereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The relative radius uncertainty\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_R/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>Repeat timing measurements and record whether successive timing\nerrors can reasonably be treated as independent. Repetition estimates\nrandom scatter but cannot repair an incorrect geometry model. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=MR^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nindependent uncertainties contribute approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_I/I\\approx\\sqrt{(u_M/M)^2+(2u_R/R)^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAvoid false precision from excessive displayed digits. Comparison with a\nreference requires agreement on rotation axis, geometry and relevant\nconditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>A difference measurement removes constant apparatus contributions but\ndoes not replace model validation or calibration.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Calorimetry: Energy Balance and\nHeat Losses<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://gpr.physik.hu-berlin.de/Praktikumsordnung/Praktikumsordnung.html\">Humboldt-Universität zu Berlin – Praktikumsordnung<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 2 sec. 1–2, lines 13–20; table of contents § 5; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L2 – Mechanics Experiments: Moment of Inertia and Oscillation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://praktikum.physik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de/versuchsbeschreibungen-physik/\">Ruhr-Universität Bochum – Versuchsbeschreibungen Physik<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · A5 lines 40–61; D3 lines 212–230; C14/C15 lines 455–499; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L2 – Mechanics Experiments: Moment of Inertia and Oscillation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M18-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M18-L3 – Calorimetry: Energy Balance and Heat Losses<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Bachelor: Physics Laboratory Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5: thermodynamics and specific heat\ncapacity; M16: sensor errors. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Predict mixing temperatures\nusing an energy balance and identify systematic heat losses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>Calorimetry relates temperature changes to energy exchanged as heat.\nFor a substance without a phase transition and with approximately\nconstant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe change is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=mc\\Delta T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn an ideally insulated mixing experiment, heat lost by the warm part\nequals heat gained by the cool part. Real experiments require additional\ninformation: the temperature field must be sufficiently uniform, probes\nmust sample it representatively, and the vessel has its own heat\ncapacity. The result is initially a model test, not a direct universal\nmaterial constant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>For two samples of the same substance with masses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_h,m_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand initial temperatures\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_h,T_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe balance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_hc(T_f-T_h)+m_cc(T_f-T_c)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_f=(m_hT_h+m_cT_c)/(m_h+m_c)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor different substances use their respective\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mc<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvalues. The vessel adds a term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{\\mathrm{cal}}(T_f-T_{\\mathrm{cal},0})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHeat exchange with the surroundings makes the energy sum for the chosen\ninternal system nonzero. Record thermal contact, duration, initial\ntemperatures and vessel assumptions.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(same material, ideal insulation)<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_f=\\frac{m_hT_h+m_cT_c}{m_h+m_c}\\quad\\text{(same material, ideal insulation)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Teaching example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof water at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\,^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmixed with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.30\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the ideal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_f=36\\,^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA lower observed equilibrium temperature could arise from a cooler\nvessel or heat loss, among other causes. It does not identify one cause\nwithout controls. A time trace\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore and after mixing is more informative than one final value; any\nfitted extrapolation needs a stated model range. Handle hot liquids only\nunder local safety instructions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_035_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Warm and cold water reaching a common ideal final temperature\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Warm and cold water reaching a common\nideal final temperature<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Energy balance for two water masses<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the ideal mixture temperature for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\,^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.30\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What are the signs of the two terms\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mc(T_f-T_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give two plausible systematic deviations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.2\\cdot60+0.3\\cdot20)/0.5=36\\,^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Negative for the initially warm part, positive for the cool\npart.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Vessel/environment heat exchange, incomplete mixing or thermometer\nlag.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>For an initial uncertainty budget, masses and initial temperatures\nmay be treated as independent only if the measurement chains justify it.\nA shared thermometer calibration error is correlated and must not be\nadded as an independent random error. Test the zero-loss model before\nadding extra fit parameters. Heat loss means energy crossing a stated\nsystem boundary; energy is not destroyed. Good analysis names that\nboundary and distinguishes measurement uncertainty from model\ninadequacy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Energy balances need explicit system boundaries and controlled\napparatus contributions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Electrical Measurements:\nCharacteristics and RC Time<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://praktikum.physik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de/versuchsbeschreibungen-physik/\">Ruhr-Universität Bochum – Versuchsbeschreibungen Physik<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · A5 lines 40–61; D3 lines 212–230; C14/C15 lines 455–499; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L3 – Calorimetry: Energy Balance and Heat Losses · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.praktikum.physik.uni-muenchen.de/grund_a/versuche/index.html\">Versuche – Praktikum Physik für LA Physik (A), Geowiss. u.a.<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Thermodynamik lines 77–81; Optik lines 94–106; Elektrizität lines 109–117; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L3 – Calorimetry: Energy Balance and Heat Losses · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M18-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M18-L4 – Electrical Measurements: Characteristics and RC Time<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Bachelor: Physics Laboratory Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: DC circuits; M16: calibration and\nuncertainty. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Account for instrument loading\nand estimate an RC time constant from a voltage trace.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>An observed current–voltage characteristic includes the measuring\ninstrument. An ideal voltmeter draws no current, whereas a real one has\nfinite input resistance. Loading can be important for high-impedance\ncircuits. An ammeter also adds resistance to the current path. Draw an\nequivalent circuit identifying source, specimen, leads and meter before\ninterpreting any measurement. Local electrical safety rules and approved\nlimits still apply at low voltage; this course does not specify the\nassembly of unverified equipment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>For an ohmic resistor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=RI<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile its response remains linear. An ideal capacitor discharging\nthrough a resistor obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm dU/\\mathrm dt=-U/(RC)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(t)=U_0\\exp(-t/\\tau)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=RC<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCharging from an ideal constant-voltage source gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_C(t)=U_0(1-\\exp(-t/\\tau))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter one time constant the charging voltage has reached\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.632<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1/e\\approx0.632<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof its final value. Cable and input capacitance can change the effective\ntime constant. Always state whether the trace represents charging or\ndischarging.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=RC,\\qquad U_C(t)=U_0\\left(1-e^{-t/\\tau}\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Synthetic example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=100\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=10\\,\\mathrm{\\mu F}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=1.00\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCharging ideally toward\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0=5.00\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.16<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_C(1\\,\\mathrm s)\\approx3.16\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDo not assess a fitted trace solely by visually overlaying it on data\npoints; also inspect residuals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{\\mathrm{obs}}-U_{\\mathrm{model}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA systematic time offset may indicate trigger delay rather than an\nincorrect resistor. Record time-zero definition and sampling rate.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_238_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Exponential rise toward five volts with time constant marked\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Exponential rise toward five volts with\ntime constant marked<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>U(t)=U₀(1−exp(−t/τ)) with τ = 1 s<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=100\\,\\mathrm{k\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=10\\,\\mathrm{\\mu F}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_C(\\tau)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_0=5\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does finite voltmeter input resistance matter?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=100000\\cdot10^{-5}=1.00\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.16<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5(1-e^{-1})\\approx3.16\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Meter input resistance loads the test device and can change voltage\nand effective time constant.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>For independent relative uncertainties in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe relative uncertainty of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=RC<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis approximately their quadrature sum. Correlated temperature dependence\nrequires a different treatment. The exponential model assumes constant\nparameters and a linear circuit. Non-exponential residuals may indicate\nleakage, instrument loading or additional elements, but cannot uniquely\ndiagnose them without controls. One fitted time constant therefore does\nnot prove a particular physical circuit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Electrical data require a documented equivalent circuit, time\nreference and residual check.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Optics Laboratory: Imaging and\nDiffraction<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://praktikum.physik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de/versuchsbeschreibungen-physik/\">Ruhr-Universität Bochum – Versuchsbeschreibungen Physik<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · A5 lines 40–61; D3 lines 212–230; C14/C15 lines 455–499; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L4 – Electrical Measurements: Characteristics and RC Time · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.praktikum.physik.uni-muenchen.de/grund_a/versuche/index.html\">Versuche – Praktikum Physik für LA Physik (A), Geowiss. u.a.<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Thermodynamik lines 77–81; Optik lines 94–106; Elektrizität lines 109–117; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L4 – Electrical Measurements: Characteristics and RC Time · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M18-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M18-L5 – Optics Laboratory: Imaging and Diffraction<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Bachelor: Physics Laboratory Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: ray optics and interference; M16:\nscale calibration. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine focal length from\nobject and image distances and diffraction angle from a grating\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>Ray and wave optics describe complementary regimes. For a thin lens\nin the paraxial approximation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/f=1/g+1/b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote positive real-object and real-image distances in the chosen\nconvention. For actual lenses the relevant reference planes need not\ncoincide with the geometric centre; principal planes, aberrations and\noff-axis rays matter when high accuracy is required. Lasers and intense\nlight sources require site-specific protective measures; this lesson\ncovers only geometric and mathematical teaching models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>For a normally illuminated grating with spacing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndiffraction maxima of order\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\sin\\theta=m\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the far field the angle can be inferred from calibrated geometry. The\nsmall-angle relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta\\approx\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napplies only for small angles. A grating with 600 lines per millimetre\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.67<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\approx1.67\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=600\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe first maximum has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta\\approx0.36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>21.1<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta\\approx21.1^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIdentify the order\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nwithout it a wavelength estimate is ambiguous.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>f<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>g<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{1}{f}=\\frac1g+\\frac1b,\\qquad d\\sin\\theta=m\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Imaging example: An object is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=0.30\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom an ideal thin lens and its real image\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.60\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbeyond the lens. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=gb/(g+b)=0.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe magnification magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|V|=b/g=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith image inversion. In actual measurements record distance origins,\nlens movement, focusing criterion and correlations between length\nreadings. Extra decimal places do not cure ambiguous focus.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_149_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two rays through a converging lens producing an inverted enlarged image\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two rays through a converging lens\nproducing an inverted enlarged image<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Object 0.30 m; image 0.60 m; focal length 0.20 m<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=0.30\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.60\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor 600 lines per millimetre.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta\\approx\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninaccurate at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mn>21<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta\\approx21^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.90<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0.30\\cdot0.60/0.90=0.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.67<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=10^{-3}/600\\,\\mathrm m\\approx1.67\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The angle is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.368<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.368<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nradians but its sine about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.36<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe approximation error grows with angle.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>Each optical model has clear limits. The paraxial lens approximation\nfails for large angular apertures, and broadband light may introduce\nchromatic effects. Grating measurements require incident-angle control,\ncalibrated geometry and attention to finite peak width as well as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhere possible compare multiple diffraction orders or object/image\npositions and inspect systematic residual patterns. Never improvise\nexperiments with high-intensity beams.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Imaging and diffraction provide different observables, each with its\nown model and angular validity range.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Spectroscopy: Wavelength\nCalibration and Resolution<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://praktikum.physik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de/versuchsbeschreibungen-physik/\">Ruhr-Universität Bochum – Versuchsbeschreibungen Physik<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · A5 lines 40–61; D3 lines 212–230; C14/C15 lines 455–499; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L5 – Optics Laboratory: Imaging and Diffraction · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.praktikum.physik.uni-muenchen.de/grund_a/versuche/index.html\">Versuche – Praktikum Physik für LA Physik (A), Geowiss. u.a.<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Thermodynamik lines 77–81; Optik lines 94–106; Elektrizität lines 109–117; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L5 – Optics Laboratory: Imaging and Diffraction · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M18-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M18-L6 – Spectroscopy: Wavelength Calibration and Resolution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Bachelor: Physics Laboratory Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: optical spectra; M15: energy\nlevels; M16: regression. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Fit a pixel-to-wavelength\ncalibration and interpret spectral resolution correctly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>A spectrometer assigns a wavelength to detector intensities. A peak\nposition initially specifies a pixel coordinate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot a physical wavelength. Known reference lines calibrate a suitable\nmodel, for example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda(p)=a+bp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover a limited range. Wide spectral spans may require nonlinear\ndispersion; however a high-order polynomial should not be chosen merely\nbecause it passes through every calibration point. Record the\nmeasurement date, reference features and valid pixel range.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>Spectral resolving power can be written\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\lambda/\\Delta\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere the definition of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be stated, for example the instrumental full width at half maximum\nof a narrow reference feature. Varying line shapes, stray light and\noverlap complicate identification. The peak width in pixels is not\nautomatically the intrinsic spectral width: the instrument response\nbroadens what is observed. Width subtraction requires a justified\nconvolution model; Gaussian and Lorentzian width rules are not\ninterchangeable.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda(p)=a+bp,\\qquad R=\\frac{\\lambda}{\\Delta\\lambda}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Synthetic two-point calibration: A 500 nm reference line falls at\npixel 100 and a 600 nm line at pixel 300. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.5\\,\\mathrm{nm/pixel}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>450<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=450\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder this model a peak at pixel 240 lies at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>570<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">570\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the instrumental line width is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda=2\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe resolving power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=300<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese calibration values are constructed for teaching and are not\nreadings from an identified spectrometer.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_266_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Linear pixel-to-wavelength calibration with peak at 570 nanometres\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Linear pixel-to-wavelength calibration\nwith peak at 570 nanometres<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Two reference lines and an unknown peak<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom references (100, 500 nm) and (300, 600 nm).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the wavelength at pixel 240?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=600\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda=2\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>450<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=450\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.5\\,\\mathrm{nm/pixel}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>450<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>570<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda(240)=450+0.5\\cdot240=570\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=600/2=300<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>Test the calibration against at least one independent line excluded\nfrom the fit. An offset in this check may indicate an unsuitable\ndispersion model or changed instrument geometry. Both reference\nwavelength uncertainty and pixel-centroid uncertainty contribute. For an\nasymmetric profile, the intensity centroid and the position of the\nmaximum may differ. Preserve raw pixels, dark-signal correction, fits,\nresiduals and calibration range. A spectral feature must not be\nidentified by colour resemblance alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Without an independent calibration check and a clearly defined\nlinewidth, a wavelength claim is fragile.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Weak Signals, Background and\nCounting Statistics<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://praktikum.physik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de/versuchsbeschreibungen-physik/\">Ruhr-Universität Bochum – Versuchsbeschreibungen Physik<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · A5 lines 40–61; D3 lines 212–230; C14/C15 lines 455–499; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L6 – Spectroscopy: Wavelength Calibration and Resolution · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.physik.fu-berlin.de/studium/lehre/fortgeschrittenenpraktikum/fp-bachelor/index.html\">Freie Universität Berlin – Methodenpraktikum / Fortgeschrittenenpraktikum für Bachelor WiSe 2026/27<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 8–38, list of experiments and prerequisites; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L6 – Spectroscopy: Wavelength Calibration and Resolution · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M18-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M18-L7 – Weak Signals, Background and Counting Statistics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Bachelor: Physics Laboratory Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: statistical physics; M15:\ndetectors; M16: uncertainty. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model signal and background\nseparately and estimate count uncertainty using Poisson statistics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>Many measurements detect discrete events such as photon arrivals,\nelectronic pulses or accepted peaks. Independent rare events at a\nstationary rate can be approximated by a Poisson count\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a fixed window, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb E[N]=\\operatorname{Var}(N)=\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDead time, time-varying rates and event correlations can invalidate that\nassumption. A weak-signal measurement records total counts, not just\nsignal counts, because background persists even when the sought source\nis absent. A dedicated background observation is part of the model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe a total count and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nan independent background count recorded for equal exposures. Estimate\nthe net count as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_s=N_t-N_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Poisson variance of this difference is the sum of variances,\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_t+N_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor unequal exposures, scale background by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=t_t/t_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_s=N_t-\\alpha N_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>α<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s^2\\approx N_t+\\alpha^2N_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese standard relations describe counting uncertainty only; exposure\ncalibration, dead-time correction and detector sensitivity require\nseparate assessment.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>α<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_s=N_t-\\alpha N_b,\\qquad u_s=\\sqrt{N_t+\\alpha^2N_b}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Constructed example with equal exposures: the total region records\n180 events and an independent background region records 40. The net\ncount is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>140<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">140<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith estimated standard uncertainty\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>180<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>14.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{180+40}\\approx14.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>140<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>14.8<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">140/14.8\\approx9.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a useful descriptive scale, but without a specified testing framework\nis neither a complete p-value nor a universal discovery claim. Choosing\nthe analysis window after inspecting data can inflate apparent evidence;\ncriteria should be defined in advance.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_054_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Bar chart showing total 180, background 40, estimated net 140\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Bar chart showing total 180, background\n40, estimated net 140<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Independent counts with equal exposure<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_t=180<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_b=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith equal exposures.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Estimate the Poisson standard uncertainty of the net count.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should a negative net estimate not be deleted\nautomatically?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>140<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_s=140<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>220<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>14.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_s=\\sqrt{220}\\approx14.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It can be a valid consequence of background noise; automatically\nremoving it would bias the analysis.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>In spectra, background and peaks may overlap; a joint fit of\nbackground shape and signal profile may be preferable to a single-window\nsubtraction. Examine residuals for temporal correlation, drift and\nsystematic peak displacement. A negative net estimate does not imply a\nnegative event count; it can result from background fluctuations when\nsignal is weak. Do not replace it with zero without a documented\nanalysis policy. Any real detector equipment and potentially hazardous\nsources require trained operators and local approval.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Background subtraction requires uncertainty from both counts and a\ntransparent window definition.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Laboratory Report, Research Data\nand Reproducibility<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://praktikum.physik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de/versuchsbeschreibungen-physik/\">Ruhr-Universität Bochum – Versuchsbeschreibungen Physik<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · A5 lines 40–61; D3 lines 212–230; C14/C15 lines 455–499; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L7 – Weak Signals, Background and Counting Statistics · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.physik.fu-berlin.de/studium/lehre/fortgeschrittenenpraktikum/fp-bachelor/index.html\">Freie Universität Berlin – Methodenpraktikum / Fortgeschrittenenpraktikum für Bachelor WiSe 2026/27<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 8–38, list of experiments and prerequisites; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L7 – Weak Signals, Background and Counting Statistics · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M18-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M18-L8 – Laboratory Report, Research Data and Reproducibility<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M18 – Bachelor: Physics Laboratory Practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M16: error budget; M17: computational\nreproducibility; M18-L1: measurement plan. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Document reports, raw data,\nscripts and limitations so that independent checking remains\npossible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research Question and Model<\/h2>\n<p>A scientific laboratory report must explain not only the final number\nbut how that claim was obtained. Essential parts include question, model\nassumptions, apparatus and safety, calibration, raw data, processing,\nuncertainties, results and discussion. Present decisive reasoning in the\nbody; long tables and complete scripts may be stored in traceable\nappendices or a linked data package. Separate observation, calculation\nand interpretation. A hypothesis modified after seeing the data must be\nidentified as post hoc rather than portrayed as the original\nexpectation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equations and Assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>Preserve raw data without overwriting it and write processed data to\nnew traceably named files. A simple package uses <code>raw/<\/code>,\n<code>processed/<\/code>, <code>analysis/<\/code> and machine-readable\nmetadata describing units, columns, equipment and calibration\nreferences. Record script version and parameter settings. If\nobservations are excluded, record the criterion, timing, affected\nrecords and effect on the result. The FAIR principles aim at\nfindability, accessibility under appropriate conditions,\ninteroperability and reusability; they do not require unrestricted\npublication of every dataset.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>12.3<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(standard uncertainty)<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(12.3\\pm0.4)\\,\\mathrm{mm},\\qquad k=1\\quad\\text{(standard uncertainty)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Example: A report states\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>12.3<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(12.3\\pm0.4)\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nexplicitly calling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na combined standard uncertainty\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nThis communicates the information more faithfully than\n<code>x = 12.327364 mm<\/code> when the apparatus does not support those\ndigits. An\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>±<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\pm u_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninterval is not automatically a 95% coverage interval. Where needed,\nexplain distributions, correlations, coverage factors and effective\ndegrees of freedom. A figure needs labelled axes with units, an\ninformative caption and a distinction between measured and simulated\npoints.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_064_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Data flow: raw data, processing, analysis and report with metadata\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Data flow: raw data, processing, analysis\nand report with metadata<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Immutable raw data and traceable processing<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should <code>raw/<\/code> and <code>processed/<\/code> be\nseparate?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>12.3<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(12.3\\pm0.4)\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot imply?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give a legitimate reason for restricting data access.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">To preserve original observations for reprocessing and audit.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It does not automatically represent 95% coverage.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example privacy, contractual or intellectual-property rights, or\nsecurity considerations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and Quality Control<\/h2>\n<p>The DFG lists subject-specific research-data recommendations, but\nretention periods and access requirements must be checked against the\napplicable institutional policies. Personal information, contractual\nrights and safety constraints may justify restricted sharing. A\nreproducible report therefore explains data access and identifies data\nthat cannot be distributed. Finally verify that each citation supports\nthe statement attached to it. A general laboratory index establishes\ncurricular context but does not validate a separately calculated\nformula.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Scientific auditability is a property of the entire data and\nreasoning chain, not of a single reported number.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://gpr.physik.hu-berlin.de/Praktikumsordnung/Praktikumsordnung.html\">Humboldt-Universität zu Berlin – Praktikumsordnung<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 2 sec. 1–2, lines 13–20; table of contents § 5; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L8 – Laboratory Report, Research Data and Reproducibility · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.dfg.de/en/basics-topics/digital-topics/research-data/recommendations\">DFG – Subject-specific Recommendations on the Handling of Research Data<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Natural sciences, lines 629–645; Overview of topics or laboratory methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M18-L8 – Laboratory Report, Research Data and Reproducibility · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
